{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of the Wikipedia page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\"**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. Hodierna was the sister of Queen Melisende of Jerusalem which gave Raymond III close ties to the royal family.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a key figure in the alliance between the County of Tripoli and the Principality of Antioch. In 1164 he participated in the Battle of Harim where he was captured by Nur ad-Din and held prisoner for nearly ten years. During his captivity Raymond IIIs cousin Bohemond III of Antioch ruled Tripoli as regent. Raymond III was released in 1173 after paying a large ransom.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nUpon his return Raymond III resumed control of Tripoli and continued to exert influence in the region. He was a supporter of King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and played a role in the succession crisis following the kings death in 1185. Raymond III was initially a candidate for the regency of Baldwin V but tensions with Guy of Lusignan led to his exclusion from power. He opposed Guys claim to the throne and supported Sibylla of Jerusalems rights. Raymond IIIs opposition to Guy culminated in his refusal to attend Guys coronation in 1186.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 likely from illness. His death marked the end of Tripolis golden age as a Crusader state. He was succeeded by his distant cousin Raymond IV of Toulouse. Raymond IIIs legacy includes his efforts to maintain stability in Tripoli and his complex political maneuvering in the volatile environment of the Crusader states. His imprisonment and eventual return highlight the precarious nature of life in the Holy Land during this period.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.17142000794410706}, {"token": "Summary", "logprob": -1.4551262855529785}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0005740663618780673}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.14250659942626953}, {"token": " Wikipedia", "logprob": -6.782778655178845e-05}, {"token": " page", "logprob": -0.4287938177585602}, {"token": " \"", "logprob": -0.1337348222732544}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -9.83428253675811e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.3378546504536644e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -4.23184028477408e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.004135509487241507}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.932505594799295e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "\"", "logprob": -0.0006422125734388828}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -6.317892984952778e-05}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06979282945394516}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -2.3245540432981215e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.6133465766906738}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.14130660891532898}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0008007657015696168}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0006926044588908553}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.8358061424805783e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.0001284993631998077}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0006739014061167836}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00013541258522309363}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.5762654542922974}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.016078339889645576}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.8412379075889476e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.376194530981593e-05}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -3.838465272565372e-05}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -3.564294092939235e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.016786862164735794}, {"token": " He", "l